### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is refuted by authoritative evidence. The World Health Organization (WHO) and the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) affirm that insecticide-treated nets (ITNs) are highly effective in reducing malaria illness and death, with no significant harms documented [CDC](https://www.cdc.gov/malaria/malaria_worldwide/reduction/itn.html). A Cochrane review provides high-certainty evidence that ITNs reduce child mortality by 17% and malaria incidence by nearly half [Cochrane](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6418392/). Even untreated nets provide protection, with one study showing a 51% reduction in *Plasmodium falciparum* infection [PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11706649/). The claim that these nets are ineffective or harmful directly contradicts global health recommendations and documented benefits.